<DocsLayout title="Specifications and evidence in Haft v9" description="Typed SpecSection lifecycle, source-currentness, evidence decay, drift, and the human gates that keep carriers separate from authority.">
  <h1>Specifications &amp; evidence</h1>

  <p>
    Haft v9 keeps specification structure, lifecycle authority, FPF source
    compatibility, implementation evidence, and release status as separate
    results. A green carrier check cannot stand in for the others.
  </p>

  <h2>Typed specification carriers</h2>

  <p>
    Project specifications are typed <code>SpecSection</code> editions. The
    standard project carriers describe the target system, the software system
    that realizes it, and the local term map. These labels are Haft
    local-practice carriers; their names do not make them FPF Core kinds.
  </p>

  <p>
    Markdown under <code>.haft/specs/</code> is a reviewable publication
    carrier. The SQL edition store remains the runtime source of truth. Use the
    public spec surfaces to draft, validate, sync, export, approve, rebaseline,
    or reopen a section; do not edit lifecycle state in SQLite by hand.
  </p>

  <h2>Read status without collapsing it</h2>

  <pre><code>haft spec status
haft spec status --json
haft spec check
haft spec check --json</code></pre>

  <p>
    <code>haft spec status</code> returns two distinct views:
    <code>workflow.state</code> reports the next onboarding or lifecycle action,
    while <code>health</code> reports structural, baseline, drift, and staleness
    findings. Workflow <code>ready</code> does not erase health findings and is
    not release evidence.
  </p>

  <h2>Draft and validate</h2>

  <pre><code>haft spec draft-contract
haft spec validate
haft spec review
haft spec sync
haft spec export ...</code></pre>

  <p>
    The drafting contract is profile-independent. Validation checks authored
    draft and active carriers without admitting them into a lifecycle state.
    Read-only semantic review is advisory. Sync imports carriers into typed SQL
    editions; export renders a deterministic markdown projection from a current
    edition.
  </p>

  <p>
    <code>haft spec check</code> is deterministic L0/L1/L1.5 structural work. It
    parses typed blocks and known carrier shapes. It does not perform L2 human
    semantic judgment or prove L3 runtime behavior.
  </p>

  <h2>Human-gated lifecycle acts</h2>

  <p>
    Approving a baseline, rebaselining after intentional semantic change, and
    reopening a section are lifecycle effects. Before asking, the agent must
    present a self-contained Human Gate Brief with the readable subject,
    affected operation, real options, immediate consequences, weakest links,
    and advisory recommendation.
  </p>

  <pre><code>haft spec approve ...
haft spec rebaseline ...
haft spec reopen ...</code></pre>

  <p>
    Preparation and explanation are not approval. A natural-language operator
    answer is sufficient only when one current brief makes the exact effect,
    subject, selection, and scope unambiguous.
  </p>

  <h2>FPF source-currentness is separate</h2>

  <p>
    When the governing FPF source changes, <code>h-spec</code> recovers the
    exact current pattern and classifies semantic fanout before editing a
    carrier. Three results remain distinct:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>compatibility with the current FPF source;</li>
    <li>current typed carrier and lifecycle state;</li>
    <li>implementation or runtime evidence for the specification's claims.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Passing one result does not promote the others. Historical binding choices
    are not rewritten merely to match current implementation prose.
  </p>

  <h2>Evidence and verification</h2>

  <p>
    Decisions and claims may carry evidence with formality, congruence, and
    freshness coordinates. Evidence can support, weaken, or fail to establish
    a claim. Expiry means the evidence is no longer current enough for the
    declared use; it does not silently make the historical observation false.
  </p>

  <p>
    Use <code>h-verify</code> when the question is whether a recorded decision
    or claim still holds. The capability compares the recorded baseline and
    predictions with current code, tests, measurements, incidents, or other
    admissible observations.
  </p>

  <pre><code>haft_refresh(action="scan")
haft_refresh(action="scan", verbose=true)
haft_query(action="evidence_path", artifact_ref="dec-...",
  evidence_ref="evid-...", attempted_use="verification reliance")</code></pre>

  <p>
    Drift, stale evidence, missing bindings, and refresh debt are attention
    signals. They do not authorize a lifecycle mutation and do not interrupt
    unrelated work already inside the accepted scope.
  </p>

  <h2>Specifications do not absorb execution policy</h2>

  <p>
    A <code>SoftwareSystemSpec</code> describes the software realization:
    responsibility allocation, behavior, interfaces, constraints, and selected
    structure. Agent policy, team policy, external-runner policy, delivery
    authority, and release policy stay outside that carrier unless their own
    exact governing object is current.
  </p>

